 22	/**
 23	 * The planet's own next upgrade: the mine that repays itself fastest, or the storage that is about
 24	 * to overflow.
 25	 *
 26	 * A player does not work down a build order. They compare what a level adds to what it costs and
 27	 * take the best ratio -- "you should prioritize mines with lowest amortization first, i.e. build the
 28	 * mines which repay their cost the fastest" -- and they stop when the next level would not repay
 29	 * itself in the time they intend to still be playing. That is the whole rule, and it is arithmetic
 30	 * over host numbers rather than a list of names: the candidates are the objects the host itself
 31	 * reports as producing resources, their price is the host's price for the next level, and what they
 32	 * add is the difference the host's own production calculation reports between this level and the
 33	 * next. An extension that adds a mine is part of this ranking the moment the host knows about it,
 34	 * and an object that adds no resource production -- a plant, a reactor, a robotics factory -- is
 35	 * simply not a candidate here, because energy has its own rule.
 36	 *
 37	 * Costs and production are compared in one currency using the trade band players use, and the
 38	 * result is a payback in hours. Two things move it, both bounded and both the account's own: a
 39	 * seeded variation whose size is the persona's skill band, so two accounts with the same numbers
 40	 * still order them slightly differently, and a nudge from the account's own remembered outcomes,
 41	 * which resolves a near-tie without ever outvoting the arithmetic or deciding legality.
 42	 *
 43	 * Storage is here rather than in a rule of its own because it is the same decision seen from the
 44	 * other side: a warehouse exists so that production does not stop while nobody is watching, and it
 45	 * is worth building exactly when it would fill inside the time the account is likely to be away.
 46	 * Its capacity comes from the host's own storage formula, so a storage object a mod adds is a
 47	 * candidate too, and a resource this planet does not produce can never fill and is skipped.
 48	 */
 49	class EconomyUpgrades
 50	{
 51	    /** How many levels of a facility the vault buys for its own sake, and the multiple of the price it keeps first. */
 52	    private const AMBITION_LEVELS = 3;
 53	
 54	    private const AMBITION_VAULT = 6.0;
 55	
 56	    /** metal-equivalent weights of the accepted trade band, which is how players compare costs. */
 57	    private const CRYSTAL_WEIGHT = 1.5;
 58	
 59	    private const DEUTERIUM_WEIGHT = 2.0;
 60	
 61	    /**
 62	     * How long a level may take to repay itself.
 63	     *
 64	     * The published range is "about 2-3 real days at any speed" for a mature account, and a young
 65	     * account is far stricter than that: a fresh mine repays itself in hours, so nothing is gained
 66	     * by accepting a week-long payback before the account has any income at all. The bound
 67	     * therefore starts at two days and relaxes with how deep the planet's production already is,
 68	     * which is the same shape another implementation settled on after measuring it.
 69	     */
 70	    private const PAYBACK_BASE_HOURS = 48.0;
 71	
 72	    private const PAYBACK_CAP_HOURS = 168.0;
 73	
 74	    private const LEVELS_PER_SLACK = 20.0;
 75	
 76	    /**
 77	     * The warehouse has to cover the gap between this account's own visits, so the gap is what the
 78	     * trigger measures against -- not a constant.
 79	     *
 80	     * A warehouse exists so production does not stop while nobody is watching, which makes the
 81	     * absence the only thing that matters: a player who opens the game five times a day is away
 82	     * about five hours and has no use for forty-eight hours of storage, while a casual player away
 83	     * half a day has every use for it. Measured against a fixed 48 hours instead, the trigger was
 84	     * true for every planet at every level -- the grand test ran the whole cohort to level-nine
 85	     * warehouses, hours from overflow yet always "urgent", while the mines underneath sat at level
 86	     * four and never got a turn. The figure is the account's, so it changes with the persona the way
 87	     * the rest of the routine does, and nothing here names a storage object.
 88	     */
 89	    private const HOURS_PER_DAY = 24.0;
 90	
 91	    /** Bounded so one remembered outcome cannot dominate the arithmetic. */
 92	    private const MAXIMUM_CASES = 20;
 93	
 94	    private const EXPERIENCE_WEIGHT_PERCENT = 20;
 95	
 96	    private const EXPERIENCE_MAXIMUM_SHARE = 0.2;
 97	
 98	    public function __construct(
 99	        private readonly ExperienceEngine $experience,
100	        private readonly BuildingQueueService $buildingQueue,
101	    ) {
102	    }
